abstract: >-
  This continued research paper extends the August 18, 2026 technical note From Model Provenance to Human Attribution by documenting the synthetic experimental program that followed. It examines how model-level provenance, lexical and semantic similarity, stylometry, timing, provider context, transformation history, and other weak signals can compose into attribution evidence; how privacy effects can change across transformation chains and orderings; and where proposed mechanisms fail to generalize. It preserves the negative v0.7 cross-family and cross-policy replication result and treats detection, provenance, attribution, identity resolution, and authority as separate system transitions.
